Core architectural principles, phased execution plan, and component definitions for the distributed AI agent project.

Instructions
AI Agent Project Guidelines
Core Principles
LLM Role: The LLM ONLY plans actions. It NEVER executes directly on the OS and NEVER handles secrets/credentials.
Capabilities: Every agent MUST declare an explicit contract of capabilities (e.g., send_message, read_events). No generic "full access" is permitted.
Risk-Based Execution: Actions are classified by risk (0-5) determining if they execute directly, require user confirmation, or are blocked.
Security: Tokens and API keys are stored in a separate secure vault, inaccessible to the LLM.
Auditability & Explicability: Every action is logged (append-only: who, what, when, authorization) and the system must be able to explain why an action was taken.
Kill Switch: A mechanism to immediately revoke permissions, sessions, and halt agents must exist.
Phased Execution Plan
Fase 0: Setup (Repo, Docker, basic environment).
Fase 1: Skeleton (Orchestrator + EchoAgent + Chat Interface).
Fase 2: Identity Gateway + Basic Policy Engine.
Fase 3: Permissions Model + Risk Levels + Confirmation flows.
Fase 4: Real Agents (Calendar, File, Telegram).
Fase 5: Auditing + Kill Switch.
Fase 6: Academic Use Cases + Robustness Testing (e.g., worker failure).
Fase 7: Assisted Profile (simplified UI + delegated permissions).
Fase 8 (Optional): Custom Mobile/Desktop UI.
Component Responsibilities
Identity Gateway: Authenticates users across platforms (Telegram, Discord).
Orchestrator: Coordinates the flow (Plan -> Policy -> Execute -> Audit). No external credentials.
LLM Planner: Translates natural language to structured intent (action + params).
Policy Engine: Evaluates intent against permissions and risk levels.
Tool Router: Routes approved actions to the specific agent.
Agents: Microservices that execute actions using specific credentials, isolated from each other.
Audit Log: Immutable record of all executions.
